# Selfie: a show that draws its own tree.
#
# Each node of the tree is a pixel: a leaf red, a stem green, a fork blue. A
# stem's child is right below it, a fork's two children further down, at the
# ends of grey diagonal edges. The board's 25 × 25 window slides over this
# picture a pixel a frame: across the top 25 rows, back across the next 25,
# and so on down, skipping stretches with nothing to show.
#
# program draws self, the tree it is given. On the show's stage, before the
# first frame, it lays the tree out (layout.lamb) and plans the window's way
# over it (tour.lamb); each frame it draws what the window shows (window.lamb).
# num.lamb and columns.lamb hold the numbers it counts with.
#
# The board does not run program but Generator.shrink of it: a much smaller
# tree that behaves the same. fix gives that tree itself as self, so the tree
# in the picture is the tree on the board.
#
# Two things shape the code:
# - The helpers used everywhere, like church, inc and dec, are bound once, in
#   program, and passed on as every definition's first arguments, the most
#   used last: naming them in each definition would put a copy in each. The
#   shrunk program unpacks itself on the board before it starts, and a
#   variable costs more there the further out it is bound; these orders made
#   the selfie smallest and quickest to start. A comment's [f x y] leaves the
#   helpers out.
# - Evaluation is eager: whatever in a function's body does not depend on its
#   arguments is computed before the function is called. So a branch is given
#   what it goes on with only once it is chosen, or every branch's rest would
#   be computed.

# [program self i]: the stage, run once, plans the window's way over self (i,
# the show's index, is not used). What it returns draws the frames: [t y x] is
# the colour of the window's pixel in row y, column x, on frame t. The helpers'
# values follow the body, innermost λ first: nz, then foldr, out to church.
program = \self
  (\church (\nl (\inc (\dec (\le (\iter (\b1 (\n31 (\cdec (\cinc (\n25 (\foldr (\nz
    \_ Pair.match (Pico.Matrix.Selfie.window nl iter b1 n25 foldr inc dec church)
      (Pico.Matrix.Selfie.tour self nz b1 cdec cinc nl le n31 n25 foldr iter inc dec church))
    (Pico.Matrix.Selfie.nz n31 dec church))
    List.foldr)
    (Pico.Matrix.Selfie.n25 iter))
    (Pico.Matrix.Selfie.cinc n31 inc))
    (Pico.Matrix.Selfie.cdec n31 dec))
    (dec b1))
    (Pico.Matrix.Selfie.b1 iter))
    Pico.Matrix.Selfie.iter)
    (Pico.Matrix.Selfie.le inc dec church))
    Pico.Matrix.Selfie.dec)
    Pico.Matrix.Selfie.inc)
    Pico.Matrix.Selfie.nl)
  Pico.Matrix.Selfie.church

# The selfie: program shrunk and given itself. Shrunk, program is rebuilt only
# when first applied, which is on the board, during the stage.
_selfie = fix (Generator.shrink program)
# The show, at 9.8 frames a second, as fast as the board manages. Each LED
# changes by at most 128 a frame towards its new colour, enough to keep up with
# a view that moves every frame.
_fps = 98
show = Pico.Matrix.show_staged 128 _fps _selfie
# Sizes in nodes: the program, and the selfie.
_to_string = \l List.join " " $ List.map Nat.to_string l
[size program; size _selfie]
# = 50835 9442

# Its first 60 frames, 6.1 s on the board:
Pico.Matrix.svg "selfie.svg" 60 show
# = selfie.svg image/svg+xml 187394 bytes sha256:3bf8119e3752000ecbbaf2e9c55c28b842b702969f80ba48c951265d43da0dcc
# The image to flash:
Pico.Matrix.flash "selfie.uf2" [show]
# = selfie.uf2 application/octet-stream 39936 bytes sha256:744169a0a030015deddd7b27cb4cf4f5dde6ceb4593379bf8e24681a1ed7d9ad

# A page that plays the window's way over the tree (page.lamb):
file "selfie.html" "text/html" $ Pico.Matrix.Selfie.page _fps _selfie
# = selfie.html text/html 48209 bytes sha256:8e54a2bd1a1bed4cc40b950f76144fe41e638ac0c557a535764fb40737aa90bf
